  "knowledgePattern": "Use a squared trigonometric identity without losing interval endpoints",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "trigonometry"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Both absolute values are nonnegative. Squaring the equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The squared identity reduces this to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so either sine or cosine is zero.</p><p>On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, the distinct candidates are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. All satisfy the unsquared equation; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> is excluded. Their sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>. Answer E.</p>",
